WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.260
What's up guys my name is Colin for theScore esports.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:11.620
You know, League of Legends fans only get two chances a year to watch major international competition.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.380
Region against region.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:21.720
From incredible comeback wins, to insane solo kills. The Mid-Season Invitational has been the gift that keeps on

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:22.220
giving.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:26.980
Speaking of which guys, don't forget to hit that sub button so we can keep on giving you

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.020
those sweet, sweet email notifications every time we publish new content.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:38.140
Now with that out of the way and MSI already underway. Let's get on with it with the Top 10

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.800
Mid-Season Invitational plays of all time.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:51.680
Kicking off our list at number 10 is WE’s 957, who, in a 1v3, pulls off the impossible.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:58.340
After getting ambushed by Flash Wolves, 957 makes an incredible play to bob, weave,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:05.280
and squeeze through a flurry of attacks, stalling just long enough for his team to take Baron.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:41.920
(Casting)

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:49.680
At number 9 we've got Febiven, whose Fnatic squad were the clear underdogs against SK Telecom T1

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.400
in MSI 2015’s semifinals.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.300
Looking to prove himself against the world’s best, a young Febiven opened things up with

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:05.740
this sick 1v1 onto Faker, before doing it again against the unkillable demon king.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:28.640
(Casting)

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:37.480
Number eight on our list goes to Huni, who in the semifinals of MSI 2015 found himself

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.220
in an unfavorable 1v2 against SKT.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:47.380
A rookie at the time, Huni showed the world what he was made of as he absolutely destroys

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.240
Faker, before finishing off MaRin.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:03:15.880
(Casting)

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.420
Coming in at number seven, is Xiye’s mid-lane Lucian at MSI 2017.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:30.040
After falling to SKT in the group stage, WE were looking to rebound in any way possible.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:37.600
Xiye did just that as he got the ball rolling in Game 2 with a gutsy tower dive onto none other than Faker.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:53.600
(Casting)

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:54.080
(Casting)

00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:07.400
(Casting)

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:12.720
At number six we've got G2’s Perkz, who, after a failed attempt to steal Baron, shows

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:20.400
us how to get away with murder as he assassinated two Gigabyte Marines players and lived to tell the tale.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:52.880
(Casting)

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:59.920
Coming in at number 5 is one of the most memorable defenses in MSI history.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.240
After losing the Elder Drake to TSM, G2 were looking to make a cheeky play for the nexus.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.800
But TSM had other plans.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:06:20.800
(Casting)

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:29.000
At number 4 we have CLG’s Stixxay at MSI 2016 who, against RNG, showed the world that

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.620
he wasn’t fazed by playing in his first International tournament.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:39.180
After losing 4 of his teammates in a late game teamfight, Stixxay avenged their deaths

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:46.080
with this jaw dropping play that saw him delete 4 RNG players, one after the other.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:07:12.880
(Casting)

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.760
Coming in at number three is Wolf’s famous Zyra play.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:23.740
On a team with an abundance of superstars, Wolf often takes a backseat to the likes of Faker and Bang.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:42.160
After splitting the first two games in the 2017 MSI grand finals, Wolf stepped up with

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:48.160
an unbelievable play that helped the Korean legends secure an MSI title.

00:07:48.160 --> 00:08:30.400
(Casting)

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:35.100
Taking the runner up spot on our list is none other than SKT’s Bang.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:42.580
In arguably one of the best matches of MSI 2015, SKT and Fnatic went back and forth in

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:47.740
a series of dramatic events that left fans on the edge of their seat.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:52.840
But there was perhaps nothing more exhilarating than Bang’s pentakill that put an end to

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:54.720
the game with style.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:25.980
(Casting)

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:34.680
Coming in at number one on our list, is a team play for the ages, it’s CLG at MSI 2016.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:42.840
Going into a crucial team fight, CLG were down 15K gold against an undefeated RNG.

00:09:42.840 --> 00:10:36.960
(Casting)

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:42.680
With a single teamfight CLG would go on to take Baron, setting themselves up for a win,

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:45.440
that fell nothing short of magical.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:11:03.640
(Casting)

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:05.540
Well guys, that's our list.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:11.900
And with SK Telecom T1 not attending MSI for the first time ever, who do you think will win?

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:15.040
Let me know in the comments below, and I will see you next week.

